" "value" "The center of the hyperbola $4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-8x+6y=11$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988753/state.json" "file_sha256" "739794044d9cba07283d372cf877f44f7ecf6f3121537242ebdb455d3eda3a98"} {"question" "q-328355" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the given equation in standard form, group the $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-8x+6y & =11 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2x)-({y}^{2}-6y) & =11 \\\\ 4[(x-1)^{2}-1]-[(y-3)^{2}-9] & =11 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-4-(y-3)^{2}+9 & =11 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-(y-3)^{2} & =11+4-9 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-(y-3)^{2} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3/2}-\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{6} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center of the hyperbola is $(\\,1,3\\,).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988753/state.json" "file_sha256" "739794044d9cba07283d372cf877f44f7ecf6f3121537242ebdb455d3eda3a98"} {"question" "q-328355" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988753/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ffd4734ecee11eae46a2ed4c95c42443f4b1ead9798dedc6e38d085c1b8ddf33"} {"question" "q-328355" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988753/state.json" "file_sha256" "739794044d9cba07283d372cf877f44f7ecf6f3121537242ebdb455d3eda3a98"} {"question" "q-328355" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988753/state.json" "file_sha256" "739794044d9cba07283d372cf877f44f7ecf6f3121537242ebdb455d3eda3a98"} {"question" "q-328355" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988753/state.json" "file_sha256" "739794044d9cba07283d372cf877f44f7ecf6f3121537242ebdb455d3eda3a98"} {"question" "q-328355" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988753/state.json" "file_sha256" "739794044d9cba07283d372cf877f44f7ecf6f3121537242ebdb455d3eda3a98"} {"question" "q-328392" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The center of the hyperbola $-{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}-32y-8x=16$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988753/state.json" "file_sha256" "739794044d9cba07283d372cf877f44f7ecf6f3121537242ebdb455d3eda3a98"} {"question" "q-328392" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in the above form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}-32y-8x & =16 \\\\ 16{y}^{2}-32y-{x}^{2}-8x & =16 \\\\ 16[{y}^{2}-2y]-[{x}^{2}+8x] & =16 \\\\ 16[(y-1)^{2}-1]-[(x+4)^{2}-16] & =16 \\\\ 16(y-1)^{2}-16-(x+4)^{2}+16 & =16 \\\\ 16(y-1)^{2}-(x+4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ \\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{1}-\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{1}-\\frac{(x-(-4))^{2}}{16} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center of the hyperbola is 